<script>
import askAssistantSource from 'crm/src/assistant/askAssistant.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import AssistantChat from 'crm/src/assistant/lib/AssistantChat.abide'
import assistantChatSource from 'crm/src/assistant/lib/AssistantChat.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import assistantChatTestSource from 'crm/src/assistant/lib/AssistantChat.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="An assistant on the site calls the CRM's handlers">
        <Example
            id="assistant-tools"
            app="crm"
            title={"`assistant(messages, provider, { mcp: true })` — the CRM's handlers as its tools"}
            summary="A rep asks the CRM's assistant where Ada works. The model here is a recording with fixed lines; the tool call it makes runs the CRM's own search handler."
            files={{
                'src/assistant/lib/AssistantChat.abide': assistantChatSource,
                'src/assistant/askAssistant.handler.ts': askAssistantSource,
                'src/assistant/lib/AssistantChat.test.ts': assistantChatTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <AssistantChat />
        </Example>

        <p><code>assistant</code> has no address of its own. You yield it from a streaming handler
            you write, so the address and the middleware are yours, and the page reads the events like
            any stream.</p>

        <p><code>mcp: true</code> hands the model the tools <code>/__abide/mcp</code> lists. Each
            call goes through the app's middleware with the asker's <code>cookie</code> and
            <code>authorization</code> headers, so the model can do what the person asking can do and
            nothing more. The handler sets <code>{'clients: { mcp: false }'}</code> so the assistant is
            not one of its own tools.</p>

        <p><code>assistant</code> keeps nothing between calls. The page sends the whole transcript
            each time, so a reload or a second server answers the next turn the same way, and every
            call pays for the transcript's tokens again.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>AssistantMessage</code></td><td>One turn of the transcript, <code>{'{ role, content }'}</code>. The last is the user's. Its <code>content</code> is its words, or its pieces in order.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>AssistantContent</code></td><td>One piece of a turn: <code>text</code>, or an <code>image</code> as base64 with its type, so a rep can show the assistant a photo of a business card. PNG, JPEG, GIF and WebP, in the rep's own turns.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>AssistantOptions</code></td><td><code>mcp</code> to offer the tools, <code>maxTurns</code>, 10 by default, to cap the model's turns in one call, and <code>signal</code> to end the conversation early, as a rep leaving ends it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>AssistantEvent</code></td><td>What the page reads: <code>text</code>, <code>tool</code> as a call starts, <code>input</code> once it is written, <code>result</code>, then <code>done</code> with the stop reason and token usage. The same events from every provider. A call to one of your handlers carries its <code>handler</code>, the <code>method</code> and <code>address</code> its import reads, and a result the <code>value</code> it answered. Import these types from <code>@abide/abide</code> in the page.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>resultsOf</code></td><td>What every successful call to one handler answered, in order: <code>resultsOf(events, searchContacts)</code> is every list of contacts the assistant searched for, typed as the handler's value.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>imagePiece</code></td><td>A pasted, dropped or picked image as a turn's piece, drawn down to 1568 pixels on its long side so a full-size screenshot is not sent with every turn. A photo is always redrawn, so where it was taken is not sent with it, and a HEIC becomes a JPEG where the browser can draw one. <code>imageFiles</code> is the images a paste or a drop carries.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Provider</code></td><td>What runs the conversation: one method, <code>run</code>, handed every turn as its pieces, yielding <code>ProviderEvent</code>s — events before the handler and the value are read — naming each tool as <code>list</code> does. Write one for another model, or a recording for a test.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Toolbox</code></td><td>What a provider is handed: <code>list</code> of the tools, and <code>call</code> to run one as the asker.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ToolAnswer</code></td><td>What <code>call</code> answers: its <code>content</code>, the same pieces as a turn — the tool's text, or the image a handler drew, which the model is shown as a picture — and whether it failed.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ToolDefinition</code></td><td>One tool in <code>list</code>: its name, description and input schema.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>abide:assistant</code></td><td>The log namespace that writes one <code>debug</code> line per conversation once it ends: the stop reason, the transcript's length, the tool calls, the tokens and the time. A conversation that ended without <code>done</code> is <code>unfinished</code>, then why: <code>aborted</code> when the request was, <code>failed</code> with the provider's message when it threw, or <code>stopped</code> when the page stopped reading.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
